# Degree of the norm equals the sum of local multiplicities

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.natDegree_norm_eq_finsum_count`
(`68d22913-543d-5543-87af-56accd991d03`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8e86cef3-7601-59a5-8e6a-6f0e0d7fd352`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $K$, and assume
that its affine coordinate ring $K[W]$ — the quotient of $K[X][Y]$ by the Weierstrass polynomial, a
free $K[X]$-algebra of rank $2$ — is a Dedekind domain. Let $a \in K[W]$ be nonzero. Then the degree
(`natDegree`) of the norm $N_{K[W]/K[X]}(a) \in K[X]$, regarded as an integer, equals the sum over
all height-one primes $v$ of $K[W]$ of the $v$-adic multiplicity $\mathrm{count}_v$ of the principal
fractional ideal generated by the image of $a$ in the function field $K(W)$, i.e. of the $v$-adic
valuation of $a$; the sum is taken as a finite sum over the (possibly infinite) index type of the
height-one spectrum, which is legitimate because only finitely many terms are nonzero. Thus the
degree of the norm of $a$ counts, with multiplicity and with no residue-degree factors since $K$ is
algebraically closed, the zeros of $a$ on the affine curve.

This is the affine half of the statement that a nonzero polynomial function on a nonsingular
Weierstrass curve has as many zeros as poles, the pole order at the point at infinity being read off
from the degree of the norm over $K[X]$. It underlies the construction of principal divisors and the
computation of valuations of Weil functions on the curve.

